const make=(slug,title,refs,cards,tasks,practice,check,model,cardViews)=>({id:'geography.a.glaciers.'+slug,topicId:'geography.a.glaciers',option:'glaciers',title,refs,cards,tasks,practice,check,model,cardViews,minutes:25});
export const glacierLessons2=[
make('valleys','Glacial troughs and hanging valleys',['1.12a: troughs; truncated spurs; hanging valleys; glacial lakes','Integrated skill 15: diagram introduction'],[
 ['Ice reshapes an existing valley','A valley glacier can widen, deepen and straighten a former river valley through abrasion and plucking. After ice loss, a broad floor and steep sides may remain: a glacial trough, often called a U-shaped valley. Real sections vary with rock structure, ice conditions and later change; not every trough is a perfect letter U.'],
 ['Spurs cut short, tributaries left high','A glacier can cut across projecting interlocking spurs, leaving steep, truncated ends. A large main-valley glacier can erode its floor more deeply than a smaller tributary glacier. After ice loss, the tributary valley may meet the main trough above its floor: a hanging valley. A stream can then descend the step as a waterfall.'],
 ['Water after ice','Over-deepening can excavate basins along a trough. Where a rock bar or deposit threshold retains water after the ice disappears, a glacial lake can remain. Later rivers, sediment deposition and slope processes keep modifying the valley. A present lake or waterfall alone does not prove a glacial origin: use the full landform setting and appropriate mapped evidence.']
],[['Predict: moving ice erodes the floor and sides of a narrow river valley. It can become…',['A wider, deeper glacial trough','A concrete flood wall','A coastal spit'],0],['A glacier cuts across projecting valley-side ridges. The ends left are…',['Truncated spurs','Point bars','Natural levees'],0],['A smaller tributary glacier erodes less deeply than the main glacier. Its valley may be…',['Hanging above the main floor after ice loss','Always deeper than the main trough','A pile of deposited sediment only'],0],['A glacial basin retains water behind a threshold after ice loss. This can form…',['A glacial lake','A glacier flowing uphill','A new volcanic intrusion'],0]],
[q('valley','p1','Which processes help excavate a glacial trough?',['Abrasion and plucking','Only zoning and road marking','Only deposition of every valley wall'],0,'Moving ice removes rock from the floor and sides.','trough'),q('valley','p2','A tributary stream drops into a much deeper main valley. A possible explanation is…',['Differential glacial erosion left a hanging valley','Every waterfall must be an oxbow','No past erosion can be involved'],0,'Main and tributary glaciers can excavate to different depths.','hanging-valley'),q('valley','p3','Why avoid identifying glaciation from one lake alone?',['Lakes have different origins; the surrounding evidence matters','Every lake is made by ice','A map never adds evidence'],0,'Connect the basin and valley setting with other evidence.','map-evidence')],
[q('valley','c1','A broad valley floor with steep sides can indicate a…',['Glacial trough','Point bar inside a bend','Natural levee'],0,'Interpret the whole section, not a perfect letter shape.','trough'),q('valley','c2','Truncated spurs form when…',['Ice erodes across projecting valley-side ridges','Sediment builds a new bank beside a river','All ridges melt into water'],0,'Erosion cuts the ends off former projections.','truncated-spur'),q('valley','c3','A hanging valley is commonly explained by…',['Greater downcutting by the main glacier than its tributary','Greater deposition of the entire main valley','The absence of gravity'],0,'The tributary floor is left higher after differential erosion.','hanging-valley'),q('valley','c4','A glacial lake may survive after ice loss if…',['An excavated basin retains water behind a threshold','Every valley drains all water instantly','A glacier still occupies the entire basin'],0,'Basin geometry and retention matter, with later changes possible.','glacial-lake')],'glacier-valley',['trough','hanging','lake']),
make('roche-moutonnee','Reading a roche moutonnée',['1.12a: roche moutonnées','Integrated skill 15: diagram introduction'],[
 ['One bedrock obstacle, two faces','A roche moutonnée is an asymmetric bedrock bump shaped by glacial erosion. The up-ice, or stoss, side is commonly gently sloping and smoothed by abrasion. The down-ice, or lee, side is steeper and more broken where plucking removes blocks. It is remaining bedrock, not simply a mound of deposited till.'],
 ['Connect form with movement','Ice and embedded debris move up and over the stoss face, smoothing it. Conditions around the lee face can favour fracture and removal of blocks along joints. The exact pattern depends on rock structure and basal conditions. The model exaggerates removal to compare processes; it is not a measured erosion rate.'],
 ['Infer direction carefully','In the typical form, ice moved from the gentler, abraded face towards the steeper, plucked face. Reverse the model: up-ice and down-ice change with flow direction; they do not always mean left and right. Use striations, surrounding landforms and geological evidence to support an interpretation of an actual site.']
],[['Predict: debris in basal ice crosses the up-ice face. It commonly…',['Abrades and smooths it','Deposits a concrete wall','Turns every rock into soil'],0],['Blocks are removed along joints on the lee side. This is…',['Plucking','Only deposition','Only plant growth'],0],['Reverse the flow. Which labels must also change?',['Up-ice and down-ice','The meaning of bedrock','The existence of gravity'],0],['For the typical shape, flow is inferred from…',['Gentler abraded face towards steeper plucked face','Always the page’s right to left','Always the steep face towards the gentle face'],0]],
[q('roche','p1','The gently sloping stoss face is commonly shaped by…',['Abrasion','Only deposition of loose till','Only urban drainage'],0,'Basal debris smooths the up-ice rock surface.','roche'),q('roche','p2','The steeper lee face commonly records…',['Plucking of jointed rock','Only deposition of all material','Only snow accumulation'],0,'Blocks can be removed from the down-ice face.','roche'),q('roche','p3','Why reverse this model?',['To separate flow-relative labels from fixed page sides','To prove all UK ice flowed west','To turn bedrock into a lake'],0,'Stoss and lee depend on the direction of former ice movement.','ice-direction')],
[q('roche','c1','A roche moutonnée is primarily…',['An eroded bedrock landform','A concrete dam','A natural levee of river alluvium'],0,'Its form is cut into bedrock rather than merely piled sediment.','roche'),q('roche','c2','In the typical form, the down-ice side is…',['Steeper and plucked','Always the gentler abraded face','Always north-facing'],0,'Describe the face relative to flow rather than compass assumptions.','roche'),q('roche','c3','A gentle abraded face is west of a steeper plucked face. Typical ice movement was…',['West to east','East to west','Impossible to infer any direction even provisionally'],0,'Use the usual stoss-to-lee relationship, supported by further evidence.','ice-direction'),q('roche','c4','Which strengthens a real-site flow interpretation?',['Consistent striations and neighbouring landform evidence','Only assuming left means upstream','Only the colour chosen for an illustration'],0,'Corroborate a shape-based inference with independent evidence.','map-evidence')],'glacier-roche',['forward','plucking','reverse']),
make('moraines','Ground and terminal moraines',['1.12b','Integrated skill 15: diagram introduction'],[
 ['Debris beneath the ice','Glaciers carry a mixture of particle sizes. Material laid down beneath ice and exposed after ice loss can form ground moraine, often a spread of till. Till is commonly poorly sorted: clay, sand and larger fragments together. Sediment redistributed by meltwater may be better sorted, so do not assume every glacial deposit is identical.'],
 ['A ridge at a former margin','Debris accumulating at a glacier’s end can form a moraine ridge through release, dumping and sometimes pushing. A terminal moraine marks an outermost limit reached during a particular advance. A margin that stays in about the same position can still receive moving ice and debris. A ridge does not mean every ice particle was stationary.'],
 ['A landscape records more than one position','As the margin retreats, pauses can leave additional recessional ridges closer to the ice source. Ground moraine and terminal ridges differ in position and form. Deposits can later be eroded or buried. Without dates and wider evidence, a sketch cannot establish the age of every ridge or the precise speed of retreat.']
],[['Predict: ice releases mixed debris beneath it. This can contribute to…',['Ground moraine','A sea arch','A new lava flow'],0],['A ridge marks the furthest margin of one advance. It is a…',['Terminal moraine','Hanging valley','Roche moutonnée by definition'],0],['A margin stays in the same position while debris arrives. A ridge can…',['Accumulate even though ice still moves','Only form if every ice particle stops forever','Never contain sediment'],0],['Successive retreat pauses can leave…',['Recessional ridges inside the former outer limit','Proof the ice flowed backwards','Only perfectly sorted marine sand'],0]],
[q('moraine','p1','Poorly sorted glacial till means…',['Different particle sizes are mixed','Every particle has identical size','It must be only dissolved material'],0,'Till often includes fine and coarse material together.','till'),q('moraine','p2','Which contrasts ground and terminal moraine?',['A broader basal deposit versus a ridge at a former outer margin','Both are always steep eroded bedrock faces','Neither can contain debris'],0,'Position, form and depositional history distinguish them.','moraine'),q('moraine','p3','A stationary ice-front position means…',['Ice supply and loss can balance while ice still flows','All ice movement must cease','The glacier necessarily moves uphill'],0,'Margin position differs from movement of individual ice particles.','ice-margin')],
[q('moraine','c1','Ground moraine commonly includes…',['Till deposited beneath former ice','Only polished exposed bedrock','Only modern concrete'],0,'It is a sedimentary deposit associated with the former glacier bed.','moraine'),q('moraine','c2','A terminal moraine records…',['An outer margin of a particular advance','The exact date of all future floods','A river’s source by definition'],0,'A former limit is evidence of extent, with dating needed for age.','terminal-moraine'),q('moraine','c3','A mixed clay–sand–boulder deposit is consistent with…',['Poorly sorted till','Perfectly sorted grains of one size','No glacial sediment under any conditions'],0,'Mixed sizes are a common characteristic, not a complete origin test alone.','till'),q('moraine','c4','Why should moraine ridges be interpreted with wider evidence?',['Different advances, later erosion and burial can complicate the record','Every ridge has its age written into this diagram','All deposits survive unchanged forever'],0,'An outline alone cannot establish a complete chronological history.','moraine-evidence')],'glacier-moraine',['ground','terminal','retreat']),
make('streamlined-landforms','Crag-and-tail and drumlins',['1.12c','Integrated skill 15: diagram introduction'],[
 ['A resistant crag shelters a tail','A resistant bedrock obstacle can survive erosion while the area down-ice is sheltered. A tapered tail of more easily eroded rock and/or sediment can remain or accumulate behind it. This crag-and-tail form reflects erosion and the preservation or deposition of material. A tail points broadly down-ice; it is not the same shape and process balance as a roche moutonnée.'],
 ['Sediment is moulded beneath ice','Drumlins are streamlined hills, commonly made largely of glacial sediment, sometimes with rock cores. In the typical form, a blunt steeper end faces up-ice and a longer taper points down-ice. Erosion, sediment deposition and deformation beneath moving ice can contribute. Formation mechanisms vary; a single animation cannot prove how every drumlin formed.'],
 ['Compare a field, not one outline','Many drumlins together can show a broad former flow alignment. The long axes indicate an alignment; a supported head-to-tail interpretation helps infer direction. Corroborate with other landforms and sediment evidence because shapes and histories vary. The roche moutonnée’s typical gentle stoss/steep lee pattern differs from the typical blunt-head/tapered-tail drumlin pattern.']
],[['Predict: resistant rock shelters material on its down-ice side. This can form…',['A crag-and-tail','A natural river levee','An oxbow lake'],0],['A typical drumlin’s tapered tail points broadly…',['Down-ice','Always north','Always towards the steeper head'],0],['Why avoid one universal drumlin-formation claim?',['Depositional, erosional and deformation histories can vary','All drumlins are concrete','Ice cannot interact with sediment'],0],['A field of aligned hills supports…',['A broad flow alignment, with other evidence for direction/history','An exact ice speed from shape alone','A date printed by the landform'],0]],
[q('stream','p1','The resistant core obstacle in a crag-and-tail is mainly…',['Bedrock','Only liquid water','Only urban pavement'],0,'The crag resists erosion and shelters the down-ice area.','crag-tail'),q('stream','p2','A typical drumlin contrasts with a roche moutonnée because it is commonly…',['Largely sediment rather than entirely an eroded bedrock bump','A heap built only by modern roads','A lake with no land surface'],0,'Composition and process balance differ; some drumlins have rock cores.','drumlin'),q('stream','p3','What can many aligned drumlins help reconstruct?',['Broad former ice-flow patterns','Exact daily temperature without data','The date of every later rockfall'],0,'Shape and alignment can inform a wider evidence-based interpretation.','ice-direction')],
[q('stream','c1','A crag-and-tail’s tail lies mainly…',['Down-ice of the resistant crag','Only inside a corrie lake','Always up-ice regardless of evidence'],0,'The resistant obstacle shelters material behind it relative to flow.','crag-tail'),q('stream','c2','A typical drumlin has…',['A blunter up-ice head and a tapered down-ice tail','A guaranteed identical shape at every site','Only a vertical cliff with no tail'],0,'Use the typical pattern with qualifications and wider evidence.','drumlin'),q('stream','c3','Which is a defensible explanation of drumlins?',['Different combinations of subglacial erosion, deposition and deformation can matter','All formed by one proven simple dumping event','None contain glacial sediment'],0,'Formation is more complex than a single universal textbook sequence.','drumlin-process'),q('stream','c4','Long axes of a drumlin field alone most directly indicate…',['An alignment that needs further evidence for secure direction and history','An exact retreat date','Only annual rainfall'],0,'Do not convert orientation into unsupported timing or speed.','map-evidence')],'glacier-streamlined',['crag','drumlin','field']),
make('upland-landuse','Farming, forestry and upland settlement',['1.13a','Integrated skill 16: diagram introduction'],[
 ['Land cover changes pathways','Grazing and machinery can compact soil, reducing infiltration and increasing quick runoff under some conditions. Heavy grazing can reduce protective vegetation, exposing soil to erosion. Effects depend on stocking, soil, slope and wetness. Farming is not one uniform surface, and not every field produces the same response.'],
 ['Forestry has a changing cycle','Well-designed woodland can intercept rainfall, support infiltration and stabilise soil. Planting disturbance, drainage, access roads and harvesting can instead increase runoff or sediment loss if poorly managed. Compare stages and management, rather than assuming all tree planting prevents floods or all forestry causes erosion.'],
 ['Settlements change surfaces and slopes','Buildings and roads add impermeable surfaces; connected drains can deliver water faster to channels. Excavation, loading or altered drainage can affect slope stability. Land-use change can therefore modify erosion, sediment delivery and runoff within an inherited glaciated landscape. Use dated OS maps, GIS and process evidence for actual places; this diagram is not such a dataset.']
],[['Predict: repeated trampling compacts wet soil. Infiltration may…',['Decrease and quick runoff increase','Always become unlimited','Stop depending on soil conditions'],0],['Compare woodland and harvesting. Why can responses differ?',['Canopy, soil disturbance and drainage differ','All trees remove gravity','Harvesting has no physical effects by definition'],0],['A new road connects hard surfaces to drains. Water may reach the river…',['More quickly','Only after becoming glacier ice','Never under any conditions'],0],['A defensible land-use comparison should use…',['Dated maps plus soil, slope and process context','Only a colour chosen in this model','An assumption every upland responds identically'],0]],
[q('land','p1','Overgrazing can increase erosion by…',['Reducing vegetation protection and exposing soil','Creating new glaciers instantly','Making every rock immune to weathering'],0,'Vegetation cover and soil condition influence sediment mobilisation.','farming'),q('land','p2','Which forestry statement includes the main trade-off?',['Woodland may reduce runoff, but poorly managed operations can increase it','Forestry always eliminates all flood risk','Road construction never disturbs soil'],0,'Distinguish established cover from the effects of operations and drainage.','forestry'),q('land','p3','Why investigate drainage when land is developed?',['It can change runoff routes and slope-water conditions','It has no relation to water','It proves all slopes must fail'],0,'Development changes processes conditionally, not by universal guarantees.','settlement')],
[q('land','c1','Soil compaction can increase rapid runoff because…',['Infiltration is reduced','Rainfall is chemically removed','Gravity ceases'],0,'Less water can enter compacted soil under comparable conditions.','farming'),q('land','c2','Which is a potential physical impact of forest harvesting and access roads?',['Soil disturbance and changed runoff or sediment delivery','Guaranteed absence of erosion','Immediate reversal of glacial history'],0,'Operations need management to protect soil and water.','forestry'),q('land','c3','A settlement adds roofs, roads and connected drains. A likely mechanism is…',['Faster delivery of runoff to channels','Automatic reduction of every flood peak','Creation of a natural moraine'],0,'Impermeable surfaces and drainage can shorten travel time.','settlement'),q('land','c4','What evidence best supports a real upland land-use investigation?',['Comparable dated OS/GIS mapping with process and local-condition evidence','Only this unscaled diagram','A universal rule that all woodland behaves alike'],0,'Mapping and environmental evidence test a location-specific explanation.','human-evidence')],'glacier-landuse',['farm','woodland','town'])
];
